        <thead><tr>
          <th>Phase</th><th>Period</th><th>Price Move</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Post-2001 Bull Run</td><td>2001–2011</td><td class="td-pos td-num">+657%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bear Market</td><td>2011–2015</td><td class="td-neg td-num">−44%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GFC Recovery Bull</td><td>2015–2020</td><td class="td-pos td-num">+77%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>COVID Spike</td><td>Mar–Aug 2020</td><td class="td-pos td-num">+40%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Post-COVID Consolidation</td><td>2020–2022</td><td class="td-neg td-num">−18%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Macro Stress Bull</td><td>2022–2026</td><td class="td-pos td-num">+125%+</td></tr>
          <tr><td>All-Time High</td><td colspan="2" class="td-hi td-num">${ath:,.0f} ({ath_date.strftime('%b %Y')})</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ SECTION 2: VOLATILITY & REGIMES ══════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s2">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">02</span>
    <span class="section-title">Volatility Analysis &amp; Regime Detection</span>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    Three distinct volatility regimes are identified using a rolling 90-day annualized volatility measure, segmented at the <strong>33rd and 67th percentiles</strong> (Low &lt; {v33*100:.1f}%, High &gt; {v67*100:.1f}%). Crisis episodes — 2008–2009 GFC, 2011 ATH reversal, 2020 COVID shock, and 2024–2026 macro stress — dominate the High-Vol regime.
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Rolling 30-Day &amp; 90-Day Annualized Volatility with Regime Bands</div>
    <div class="regime-legend">
      <div class="rl-item"><div class="rl-dot" style="background:rgba(91,155,213,0.25)"></div>Low Vol (&lt;{v33*100:.1f}%)</div>
      <div class="rl-item"><div class="rl-dot" style="background:rgba(224,140,58,0.25)"></div>Medium Vol</div>
      <div class="rl-item"><div class="rl-dot" style="background:rgba(224,85,85,0.25)"></div>High Vol (&gt;{v67*100:.1f}%)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:300px">
      <canvas id="volChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Regime Performance Statistics</div>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr>
        <th>Regime</th><th class="td-num">Trading Days</th><th class="td-num">% of Period</th>
        <th class="td-num">Ann. Return</th><th class="td-num">Ann. Volatility</th><th class="td-num">Sharpe (rf=0)</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
"""

for _, row in regime_stats.sort_values('ann_vol').iterrows():
    days = int(row['count'])
    pct = days / len(df) * 100
    ann_r = row['ann_ret']
    ann_v = row['ann_vol']
    sh = ann_r / ann_v if ann_v != 0 else 0
    r_class = 'td-pos' if ann_r > 0 else 'td-neg'
    regime_color = {'Low Vol': '#5B9BD5', 'Medium Vol': '#E08C3A', 'High Vol': '#E05555'}.get(row['regime'], '')
    html += f"""        <tr>
          <td><span style="display:inline-block;width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:2px;background:{regime_color};margin-right:6px"></span>{row['regime']}</td>
          <td class="td-num">{days:,}</td>
          <td class="td-num">{pct:.1f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num {r_class}">{ann_r:+.1f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num">{ann_v:.1f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num">{sh:.3f}</td>
        </tr>
"""

html += f"""      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key finding:</strong> The High-Vol regime accounts for roughly one-third of trading days but concentrates most of gold's crisis-driven gains. The Low-Vol regime exhibits the highest risk-adjusted returns, consistent with calm accumulation phases. Volatility clustering is pronounced — once elevated, it tends to persist.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ SECTION 3: SEASONAL PATTERNS ══════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s3">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">03</span>
    <span class="section-title">Seasonal &amp; Calendar-Based Patterns</span>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    Monthly seasonality is assessed using average daily log returns per calendar month, tested for statistical significance via a t-test against zero. <strong>January, February, and August</strong> show the most consistently positive average returns. Statistical significance (p &lt; 0.10) is limited, consistent with gold being a macro-driven, not calendar-driven, asset.
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Average Monthly Return (annualized %) by Calendar Month</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:240px">
        <canvas id="seasonChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Average Daily Return by Day-of-Week (%)</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:240px">
        <canvas id="dowChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Monthly Seasonality — Statistical Summary</div>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr>
        <th>Month</th>
        <th class="td-num">Avg Daily Ret</th>
        <th class="td-num">Ann. Equiv.</th>
        <th class="td-num">Std Dev</th>
        <th class="td-num">T-Stat</th>
        <th class="td-num">P-Value</th>
        <th class="td-num">Significant?</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
"""

for m in range(1, 13):
    row = monthly_avg.loc[m]
    sig = '✓ p<0.10' if row['p_value'] < 0.10 else '—'
    sig_class = 'td-pos' if row['p_value'] < 0.10 and row['mean'] > 0 else ('td-neg' if row['p_value'] < 0.10 else '')
    ret_class = 'td-pos' if row['mean'] > 0 else 'td-neg'
    html += f"""        <tr>
          <td>{month_names[m-1]}</td>
          <td class="td-num {ret_class}">{row['mean']*100:+.4f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num {ret_class}">{row['ann_ret_pct']:+.2f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num">{row['std']*100:.4f}%</td>
          <td class="td-num">{row['t_stat']:+.3f}</td>
          <td class="td-num">{row['p_value']:.3f}</td>
          <td class="td-num {sig_class}">{sig}</td>
        </tr>
"""

html += f"""      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Monthly Return Heatmap — Avg Daily Log Return (%) by Year &amp; Month</div>
    <div id="heatmap-container"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ SECTION 4: RETURN DISTRIBUTION ══════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s4">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">04</span>
    <span class="section-title">Return Distribution &amp; Tail Risk</span>
  </div>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-pill">Mean daily return <span>{rets.mean()*100:+.4f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Median <span>{rets.median()*100:+.4f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Skewness <span>{skew:+.3f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Excess Kurtosis <span>{kurt:.3f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">VaR 95% <span>{var_95*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">VaR 99% <span>{var_99*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">CVaR 95% <span>{cvar_95*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">CVaR 99% <span>{cvar_99*100:.3f}%</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    Gold daily returns exhibit <strong>positive skewness ({skew:+.3f})</strong> and <strong>excess kurtosis of {kurt:.2f}</strong> (fat tails), meaning large positive moves occur more frequently than a normal distribution would predict. VaR at 99% is <strong>{var_99*100:.2f}%</strong> — worst 1% of trading days lose more than this. CVaR (expected shortfall) at 99% is <strong>{cvar_99*100:.2f}%</strong>.
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Daily Return Distribution vs Normal Curve</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:280px">
