</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ SECTION 4: RETURN DISTRIBUTION ══════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="s4">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">04</span>
    <span class="section-title">Return Distribution &amp; Tail Risk</span>
  </div>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-pill">Mean daily return <span>{rets.mean()*100:+.4f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Median <span>{rets.median()*100:+.4f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Skewness <span>{skew:+.3f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Excess Kurtosis <span>{kurt:.3f}</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">VaR 95% <span>{var_95*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">VaR 99% <span>{var_99*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">CVaR 95% <span>{cvar_95*100:.3f}%</span></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">CVaR 99% <span>{cvar_99*100:.3f}%</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    Gold daily returns exhibit <strong>positive skewness ({skew:+.3f})</strong> and <strong>excess kurtosis of {kurt:.2f}</strong> (fat tails), meaning large positive moves occur more frequently than a normal distribution would predict. VaR at 99% is <strong>{var_99*100:.2f}%</strong> — worst 1% of trading days lose more than this. CVaR (expected shortfall) at 99% is <strong>{cvar_99*100:.2f}%</strong>.
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Daily Return Distribution vs Normal Curve</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:280px">
